{
  "about": {
    "what": "How squad age, caps, big-five league players, squad market value, Elo rating and playing at home went with results at the 2026 World Cup. Associations on one tournament, not causes.",
    "matches": 104,
    "teams": 48,
    "squads": "Final squads (26 players). Age on 2026-06-11; caps before the tournament; big-five clubs are those in the Premier League, La Liga, Bundesliga, Serie A or Ligue 1 at call-up.",
    "results": "Win, draw or loss after extra time; a shootout counts as a draw.",
    "model": "Symmetric ordered logit on the differences between the two teams (scripts/analysis/ordered_logit.py). Odds ratios are per the unit given, for the first team doing better (a win rather than a draw, or a draw rather than a loss); odds_ratio_per_sd is per standard deviation of the match-level difference. leave_one_out_rps forecasts each match from a model fitted to the other 103 (lower is better); *_minus_elo_only below zero means better forecasts than elo_only, with a 95% bootstrap interval over matches.",
    "factors_fixed_in_advance": "The factor list was fixed before the models were fitted. Squad market value was on it from the start and was added when its data arrived.",
    "market_value": "Transfermarkt values set in the 12 months before the tournament (data/raw/transfermarkt_wc2026_snapshot.csv, from market_values.py). The factor is the combined value of each team's 11 most valuable players, on a log2 scale, so its odds ratio is per doubling. players_valued counts the players with a recent value; Iran has 9, so its figure is low.",
    "team_level": "Spearman correlations over the 48 teams with 95% bootstrap intervals.",
    "limits": "One World Cup is 104 matches, so only strong effects can show, and the factors overlap (see factor_overlap). In all_factors, age and caps move together, so their separate coefficients are not reliable."
